body: "## What happens\n\nThe \"Merge release metadata through protected PR\" step of the fleet release workflow (canonical `.github/workflows/release.yml` here, copied into about 37 packages) blames workflow approval for every release PR that times out after any of its runs was once `action_required`. On 2026-11-11 five Daily Release runs ended with:\n\n```\nRelease PR #N did not merge within 30 minutes because its CI run is awaiting workflow approval (last state: BLOCKED). Approve the run linked above once; ...\n```\n\nWhat was actually wrong, with no run awaiting approval at that point:\n\n| Release PR | Real state |\n| --- | --- |\n| unbraind/pm-web#316 | CI ran and failed (`test (22)`, `test (26)`) |\n| unbraind/pm-graph#207 | CI ran and failed (`test (22)`, `test (26)`, coverage gate) |\n| unbraind/pm-ado#104 | checks green, branch BEHIND main |\n| unbraind/pm-vcs#196 | checks green, branch BEHIND main |\n| unbraind/pm-rl#164 | merge conflicts (DIRTY) |\n\n## Cause\n\n`awaiting_approval=1` is set the first time the loop sees an `action_required` run on the release commit, and it is never cleared. Once that run is approved (by the step's own `approve` call or by a maintainer) and then fails, or the branch falls behind, the deadline message still takes the approval branch. The accurate message (`failing: …`, `still settling: …`) is never printed. The wrong diagnosis cost a night of triage (\"waiting for a runner\", then \"awaiting approval\").\n\n## Expected\n\n- **Recompute at the deadline.** Report approval only for runs that are still `action_required` or `waiting` at that moment.\n- **Name the real state otherwise:** failing check names with their run links, `BEHIND` (strict up-to-date: update the branch), `DIRTY` (regenerate the release metadata) or still settling.\n- **Tests that fail on revert:**\n  - approved-then-failed reports the failing checks;\n  - still-waiting reports approval;\n  - BEHIND and DIRTY each report their state and remedy.\n\nPropagation to the packages should go through the existing template path, not through hand copies.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-11-12T12:00:46Z)\n>\n> <!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n> \n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-ops#250 - Free release runners while protected PR checks run [closed]\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\":\"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
